Both are web fonts served by the browser client, which builds them into its
bundle under a content-hashed name. The OFL permits bundling and redistribution
with the software, provided the license text travels with the fonts and neither
is sold on its own. That is why each family's OFL.txt is committed in the same
directory as its font file rather than referenced from afar — and why a
byte-identical copy also sits under web/public/, which is the tree the build
copies verbatim, so the DISTRIBUTED bundle carries the grant and not only the
source tree does. ASSETS.md records the same fact where a reader looking for
asset provenance will find it.
